// ALG1 Unit 06 Lesson 01 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Solving Systems by Graphing
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.AR.5

::ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
The lines y = x + 2 and y = -x + 6 intersect at (2, 4). What is the solution of the system?
{
~A. x = 2#This gives only the x-coordinate. A system solution must be an ordered pair.
=B. (2, 4)#Correct. The intersection point is the ordered pair that satisfies both equations.
~C. (4, 2)#This reverses the x- and y-coordinates of the intersection.
~D. y = 4#This gives only the y-coordinate, not the full system solution.




}

::ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
Which point satisfies both y = 2x + 1 and y = -x + 7?
{
~A. (1, 3)#This works in y = 2x + 1 but not in y = -x + 7.
~B. (3, 4)#Substitution gives 7 in the first equation, not 4.
~C. (0, 7)#This is on y = -x + 7 but not on y = 2x + 1.
=D. (2, 5)#Correct. 5 = 2(2) + 1 and 5 = -2 + 7.




}

::ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
What does the intersection of two lines represent in a system of equations?
{
=A. The ordered pair that satisfies both equations#Correct. Both graphs share that point, so both equations are true there.
~B. The y-intercept of the first equation#A y-intercept may be on only one line, not necessarily both.
~C. The slope of both equations#Slope describes steepness, not the shared solution point.
~D. The larger y-value on the graph#The solution depends on both equations, not the largest y-value shown.




}

::ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
The equations y = 3x + 1 and y = 3x - 4 are graphed together. What type of solution does the system have?
{
~A. One solution#Lines with the same slope and different intercepts do not intersect.
~B. Infinitely many solutions#That would happen if the equations represented the same line.
=C. No solution#Correct. The lines are parallel because they have the same slope and different y-intercepts.
~D. The solution is (0, 1)#This is the y-intercept of one line, not an intersection of both lines.




}

::ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L01_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5]
A graph shows two lines crossing at (4, 1). Which statement is true?
{
~A. Only x = 4 must satisfy both equations#Both coordinates are needed to check a system.
=B. The ordered pair (4, 1) satisfies both equations#Correct. The crossing point belongs to both lines.
~C. The system has no solution#A crossing point means there is one solution.
~D. The two equations must be identical#Identical equations overlap completely, not just at one crossing point.




}

